    "visibleText": "Why should you learn writing C extension? - PyCon HK Skip to content Menu PyCon HK Conference Highlights Schedule 2024 2023 2022 2021 2020 Fall 2020 Spring 2018 2017 2016 2015 Sponsors 2024 2023 2022 2021 2020 Fall 2018 2017 2016 2015 Communities 2024 2023 2022 Booths 2024 2023 Organizers 2024 2023 2022 Volunteers 2024 2023 2022 2021 2020 Fall 2018 2017 2016 2015 PyCon HK Why should you learn writing C extension? Posted on April 29, 2020 November 6, 2020 Why should you learn writing C extension? (Gavin Chan) (Hong Kong) is an English session in the online PyCon HK 2020 Spring. Are you sometimes frustrated by the performance of Python? Do you always look for open source library to speed up your process? Do you feel numpy cannot give you much edge to enhance the performance now? If you answer yes in any of the above questions, probably writing C extension will be a great solution to you. First we will go through the reasons to learn writing C extension. Then the modern libraries, e.g. Cython and pybind11, will be introduced to develop C extension and compared with their features and functionalities. Along the way, a few simple but common problems are demonstrated that writing C extension can be straightforward, and improves greatly your software quality and performance. Slide: https://github.com/pyconhk/pyconhk-presentations/blob/master/2020spring/Why_should_you_learn_writing_C_extension.pdf Level: Intermediate Speaker Bio: Gavin Chan Gavin Chan is a principal quantitative developer in AXA Investment Manager Chorus Ltd with 7+ years of experience in software development and finance industry. https://github.com/gavincyi https://www.linkedin.com/in/gavin-ying-in-chan-43b00127/ Session Time in HKT: 4:00 PM on 8 May 2020 Friday. Session Time in UTC: 8:00 AM on 8 May 2020 Friday. Instagram LinkedIn Facebook Twitter YouTube Search for: Archives Archives Select Month November 2024 October 2024 September 2024 July 2024 April 2024 December 2023 October 2023 September 2023 July 2023 April 2023 November 2022 October 2022 August 2022 October 2021 September 2021 May 2021 November 2020 October 2020 September 2020 May 2020 April 2020 November 2018 October 2018 September 2018 August 2018 November 2017 October 2016 November 2015 History 2026 2025 2024 2023 2022 2021 2020 Fall 2020 Spring 2020 2018 2017 2016 2015 ©2026 PyCon HK | Powered by SuperbThemes! ← A Day Has Only 24±1 Hours ← Why is it so difficult to retire an unused function? (Gavin Chan) (Hong Kong)"
